{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014pRGzLYRTvzQKLAeftF3rn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand the current state and determine what tasks to propose.\n\n## Project Summary\n\nThis is a Go reimplementation of GNU coreutils/moreutils utilities, verified via differential testing. The pipeline is: extract specs from C source → specify (SRD) → synthesize (code gen) → verify (differential testing).\n\n## Current State\n\nLooking at the `requirement_states` field, I can see which SRDs have requirements in \"ready\" state. Let me list them:\n\n- srd004-ts: All requirements ready (R1.1-R10.3)\n\n\n- srd097-who: R2.1-R3.3 ready\n- srd099-shred: R1.1-R3.3 ready\n- srd100-chroot: R1.1-R2.3 ready\n- srd101-install: R1.1-R3.3 ready\n- srd102-tsort: R1.1-R2.3 ready\n- srd103-pathchk: R1.1-R2.3 ready\n- srd104-test: R1.1-R4.2 ready\n- srd105-stty: R1.1-R7.3 ready\n- srd106-df: R1.1-R4 ready\n- srd107-dir: R1.1-R2.4 ready\n- srd108-vdir: R1.1-R2.4 ready\n- srd109-dircolors: R1.1-R3.5 ready\n- srd110-pr: R1.1-R5.2 ready\n- srd111-ptx: R1.1-R5.2 ready\n- srd112-chronic: R1.1-R2.3 ready\n- srd113-pee: R1.1-R2.2 ready\n- srd114-vidir: R1.1-R2.2 ready\n\nMost of these utilities are already implemented across their respective release ranges. I'm checking which ones still need work by looking at their code status.\n\nNow I'm filtering for utilities marked as \"not started\" that also have ready requirements, since those are the ones I should prioritize. Looking through the list, most of the unstarted utilities don't have any ready requirements yet, so they're not blocking anything immediately. I'm continuing through the release roadmap and noticing that most items still lack ready requirements, but starting around rel11.1 with the `who` utility, some entries are beginning to have their dependencies satisfied—specifically `srd097-who`, `srd099-shred`, and `srd100-chroot`. I'm seeing the rest of the release checklist with all the remaining items marked as having ready requirements. Now I need to identify which release to tackle first based on the priority system—looking at the earliest incomplete one where the blocked items actually have their dependencies satisfied. Continuing through the remaining modules, I see several more utilities are blocked on missing dependencies—expand and unexpand need prerequisites, cut and paste are waiting on their requirements, and the hashing functions are split between implemented and blocked. Moving into file operations, mkdir and rmdir are done but mktemp is stuck, while the environment and user info commands are mostly complete except groups. The sort utility hasn't started yet, but tr and tail are implemented. Looking at the dependencies more carefully, I see that 11.1 has some ready requirements with the `who` command, and 12.0 has several commands marked as ready too. But I need to understand the actual dependency structure better—just because a command is marked ready doesn't mean its release is unblocked. Let me check what the roadmap actually says about release dependencies to figure out which one I should focus on next. , pr, ptx have ready requirements too. So 11.1 is the earliest release with incomplete work and ready requirements—specifically the who utility. Checking its requirements, all seven of them (R2.1 through R3.3) are marked ready. I need to verify the weight budget before proposing tasks, but I don't have direct access to the requirements file, so I'll need to read it to get the weight information. that there's no R1.x requirements in the ready state suggests they've already been completed. For the `who` command, I have 7 ready requirements spread across R2 and R3, so I'll need to split them into multiple tasks respecting the 4-requirement weight limit per task. I should also follow the contract pattern by proposing a contract task first that defines all the expor