es exist in docs/specs/software-requirements/, but I can't read them directly. I think the right approach is to reference the SRD files in the task descriptions and let the stitch agent pull the details. I can infer the general scope from the roadmap descriptions and the utilities' purposes, then point to the specific requirement IDs for the agent to expand on. I'm realizing I can't actually read the requirements.yaml file to check individual requirement weights, so I'll need to be conservative and assume each requirement has a weight of 1. That keeps me at the 4-requirement maximum per task. If some requirements do have higher weights, the orchestrator will catch that and adjust accordingly. I should focus on structuring the tasks properly and let the system validate the weights rather than guessing.\n\nNow I need to apply the constraint about requirement_states—I should only propose tasks for requirements marked as 'ready'. Looking at the ready items for cmd/who, I can group them into two tasks: one covering the extended session display requirements, and another for error handling and differential tests.\n\nFor the next release cycle, I'm evaluating 12.0 versus 12.1. Since 12.0 comes first in the priority order and both depend on the same partially-completed foundation, I'll focus there. The three new packages—shred, chroot, and install—each have substantial requirement lists, but chroot stands out as the simplest with only six ready requirements. I'll start by drafting its contract, then move into the initial implementation tasks for its core functionality. depends on contract)\n\nBut wait - that leaves R2.1, R2.2, R2.3 for a future batch. That's fine since we can only do 4 tasks.\n\nActually, looking at this differently - maybe I should do tsort from 12.1 instead, since it's simpler (7 requirements).\n\nHmm, but 12.0 comes first in release order. Let me stick with 12.0.\n\nLet me reconsider: chroot is really simple (changes root directory and runs a command). 6 requirements. Contract + 2 implementation tasks = 3 tasks. But I only have 2 slots.\n\nActually for contracts, they're usually small. The contract just defines exported types/functions. For chroot, the contract might be trivial - chroot might not even have exported types (it's a cmd/ package with just a main function).\n\nWait - cmd/ packages don't need contracts at all! They don't export anything, just a main function. Looking at the existing tasks in the log, cmd/ packages like the stitch who implementation skip contracts entirely. The package_contracts in the prompt only list pkg/ packages anyway, not cmd/ packages.\n\nSo I can skip the contract requirement for cmd/ packages. That means with my 2 remaining slots, I can propose 2 implementation tasks for 12.0 or 12.1 utilities.\n\nchroot has 6 requirements - I could split it into 2 tasks covering the core functionality and error handling/tests. tsort has 7 requirements. Either way works with 2 slots.\n\nLet me go with chroot since it comes first in 12.0. Task 1 covers R1.1-R1.3 (core implementation), Task 2 covers R2.1-R2.3 (error handling and tests). Now I need to draft the full task descriptions following the naming convention from the git log.\n\nFor required_reading, the stitch agent needs the SRD, any existing code in cmd/, CLAUDE.md for project rules, and pkg/ dependencies. Since chroot is new, I'll include the SRD and CLAUDE.md. For the files field, I need to think about what already exists versus what will be created. I'm sketching out the task structure with the four requirements, but I realize I don't actually have the specific details of what R2.1 through R2.4 entail—I should just reference the SRD document and let the stitch agent pull those details. Looking at similar tasks in the git log, I can see the pattern for how these are structured, so I'll keep the task definition generic and focused on the files that need modification.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the constraint about splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks versus combining trivial changes. With seven who requirements and six chroot requirements across a maximum of four tasks, I need to figure out how to balance these constraints without exceeding the limit.\n\nLooking at the precedent in the git log, tasks for cmd/ packages seem to combine implementation and differential tests together since they're tightly coupled in the same test file. The \"split implementation and tests\" rule probably applies more to pkg/ packages where tests might be more independent. Given that constraint, I think combining implementation and tests for cmd/ packages in single tasks is the practical approach here. R3.1, R3.2, R3.3)\nTask 2: cmd/chroot core implementation (srd100 R1.1, R1.2, R1.3)\nTask 3: cmd/chroot error handling and differential tests (srd100 R2.1, R2.2, R2.3)\n\nAll four tasks stay within the weight limit of 4, and the dependency chain flows from the who command work into the chroot implementation.\n\nTask 1 depends on Task 0 since the error handling requirements likely build on the foundational features, while Task 2 stands independently, and Task 3 requires the chroot core to be completed first. The R2.x and R3.x groupings suggest these are distinct feature areas, with R3.x providing validation and error handling for what R2.x establishes.\n\nNow I'm identifying the key references I'll need for the YAML: the SRD documents for both utilities, the existing implementation files, test suites, and project conventions. Since the roadmap mentions that all utilities read the utmpx database and are verified against GNU coreutils, I should make sure those dependencies are clear in the task definitions.\n\nWriting the YAML output... I should reference the SRD requirements by ID and let the stitch agent handle the implementation details based on those specifications. Now I'm structuring the task with proper YAML formatting that includes the deliverable type, required reading materials, and file modifications needed to implement the extended session display features.\n\nWriting task YAML... I'm filling in the remaining design decision about using file metadata for idle time calculations, then laying out the acceptance criteria to validate the implementation against the srd097-who specification and the reference GNU who binary through differential testing. I need to stick to design decisions that actually come from the SRD and architecture rather than making assumptions. The shared protocols from the architecture are solid—using the differential testing framework, syscall abstractions, signal handling, environment variables for tests, and the reference binary lookup approach. Those are what I should base the design decisions on. And I should match the task title format from the git log to keep things consistent.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the specific SRD sub-requirement IDs. Each of my task requirements needs to reference the actual SRD requirement with its sub-ID, like srd097 R2.1, R2.2, and so on. That way the tasks are properly anchored to the requirements document and I'm not exceeding the four sub-requirement limit per task.\n\nI should also double-check the constraints—no file reading or command execution, everything comes from the project context provided. And for chroot specifically, I'm noting that it requires elevated privileges which could complicate differential testing, though the SRD requirements should account for that scenario.\n\nGiven that chroot has the fewest requirements among the 12.0 utilities and is conceptually straightforward, I'll move forward with that choice for the second task pair.\n\nNow I'm structuring the final output with the who implementation task, making sure the mapping to specific SRD requirements is explicit in the title and description. I'm also setting the locale to C in the test environment to ensure consistent output, and I'll skip the tests if the gwho reference binary isn't available. The acceptance criteria cover full implementation of the R2.1 through R2.4 requirements, successful compilation, passing tests, and differential test validation against gwho. Next I'm looking at error handling and differential tests for the R3.1 through R3.3 requirements. I'm laying out the design approach: using differential testing against the reference gwho binary, checking for its availability and skipping if needed, and setting the locale to C for consistent output. The acceptance criteria cover full implementation of the R3.x requirements, successful compilation and test execution, and passing all differential tests for error handling cases.\n\nNow I'm moving on to the chroot implementation task, which requires creating the main chroot command that changes the root directory and executes a command, along with differential tests against the gchroot reference binary. I need to implement the R1.1 through R1.3 requirements from the specification. Now I'm moving on to implement error handling and differential tests for the R2.x requirements, which means modifying the main chroot implementation and test suite to cover additional error cases and edge conditions specified in the requirements document. I'm realizing that cmd/ packages are main packages without exported types or interfaces, so they don't need contract tasks—that requirement only applies to library packages in pkg/. I should also reconsider the task dependencies: the who and chroot tasks target different packages, so they can run in parallel since neither depends on the other.\n\nNow I'm putting together the final response with the project state summary, priority reasoning, weight budget verification, and the YAML task list, making sure each task description is self-contained with all the context the agent needs to implement it successfully.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":45474,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_inpu